NCT02520063_36735117,Number of Participants Who Experienced Dose-limiting Toxicities [Time Frame: 4 weeks],"Fifteen patients enrolled. The recommended phase 2 dose of everolimus in combination with letrozole and carotuximab was 10 mg PO daily. The most frequent adverse events were headache (67%), fatigue (47%), facial flushing and swelling (47%), gingival hemorrhage (40%), epistaxis (33%), nausea and vomiting (27%). Headache constituted a dose-limiting toxicity. At least two signs of mucocutaneous telangiectasia developed in 92% of patients. Carotuximab accumulated in the extravascular space and accelerated the biodistribution and clearance of everolimus. All patients had residual disease. Gene expression analyses were consistent with downregulation of genes involved in proliferation and DNA repair. Among 6 patients with luminal B breast cancer, 5 converted to luminal A after one cycle of therapy.",0.4191,58.09,llm,gpt-4o-mini,concordant,The registered primary outcome regarding dose-limiting toxicities is clinically equivalent to the published primary outcome that identifies headache as a dose-limiting toxicity. Both outcomes assess the same clinical construct of dose-limiting toxicities.,high,True,False,False,False,,1.0,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Headache constituted a dose-limiting toxicity.,,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Number of Participants Who Experienced Dose-limiting Toxicities [Time Frame: 4 weeks]"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Headache constituted a dose-limiting toxicity."", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint_reported"": true, ""additional_endpoint_present"": false, ""additional_endpoint_disclosed"": false, ""change_disclosed"": null, ""actual_change_evidence"": [], ""classification_label"": ""Concordant (no change)""}]",no,,,,,,,pipeline_v4.5,2026-09-05T04:50:33.056721+00:00
