NCT03206203_38095878,Progression Free Survival (PFS) [Time Frame: Up to 3 years.],"All 106 patients with metastatic TNBC who were enrolled were female with a mean (range) age of 55 (27-79) years, of which 12 (19%) identified as African American/Black, 1 (1%) as Asian, 73 (69%) as White, and 11 (10%) as unknown. Patients were randomized and received either carboplatin (n = 50) or carboplatin and atezolizumab (n = 56). The combination improved PFS (hazard ratio [HR], 0.66; 95% CI, 0.44-1.01; P = .05) from a median of 2.2 to 4.1 months, increased ORR from 8.0% (95% CI, 3.2%-18.8%) to 30.4% (95% CI, 19.9%-43.3%), increased CBR at 6 months from 18.0% (95% CI, 9.8%-30.1%) to 37.5% (95% CI, 26.0%-50.6%), and improved OS (HR, 0.60; 95% CI, 0.37-0.96; P = .03) from a median of 8.6 to 12.6 months. Subgroup analysis showed PD-L1-positive tumors did not benefit more from adding atezolizumab (HR, 0.62; 95% CI, 0.23-1.65; P = .35). Patients with high TILs (HR, 0.12; 95% CI, 0.30-0.50), high mutation burden (HR, 0.50; 95% CI, 0.23-1.06), and prior chemotherapy (HR, 0.59; 95% CI, 0.36-0.95) received greater benefit on the combination. Patients with obesity and patients with more than 125 mg/dL on-treatment blood glucose levels were associated with better PFS (HR, 0.35; 95% CI, 0.10-1.80) on the combination. TNBC subtypes benefited from adding atezolizumab, except the luminal androgen receptor subtype.",0.3895,61.05,llm,gpt-4o-mini,concordant,"The registered primary outcome of Progression Free Survival (PFS) is reported as the primary outcome in the publication without any changes in construct, definition, or population. Therefore, the outcomes are concordant.",high,True,False,False,False,,1.0,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,Progression Free Survival (PFS),,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Progression Free Survival (PFS)"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Progression Free Survival (PFS)"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": null, ""same_definition"