NCT02441946_31615937,"Percent Change From Baseline to 2 Weeks in Ki67 Expression [Time Frame: Baseline, 2 Weeks]","Abemaciclib, alone or in combination with anastrozole, achieved a significant decrease in Ki67 expression and led to potent cell-cycle arrest after 2 weeks of treatment compared with anastrozole alone. More patients in the abemaciclib-containing arms versus anastrozole alone achieved complete cell-cycle arrest (58%/68% vs. 14%, P < 0.001). At the end of treatment, following 2 weeks lead-in and 14 weeks of combination therapy, 46% of intent-to-treat patients achieved a radiologic response, with pathologic complete response observed in 4%. The most common all-grade adverse events were diarrhea (62%), constipation (44%), and nausea (42%). Abemaciclib, anastrozole, and the combination inhibited cell-cycle processes and estrogen signaling; however, combination therapy resulted in increased cytokine signaling and adaptive immune response indicative of enhanced antigen presentation and activated T-cell phenotypes.",0.3436,65.64,llm,gpt-4o-mini,concordant,"The registered primary outcome of percent change in Ki67 expression from baseline to 2 weeks is reported in the publication as a significant decrease in Ki67 expression after 2 weeks of treatment, indicating that the primary endpoint is the same clinical construct and timeframe.",high,True,False,False,False,,1.0,Concordant (no change),clinically equivalent,"Abemaciclib, alone or in combination with anastrozole, achieved a significant decrease in Ki67 expression and led to potent cell-cycle arrest after 2 weeks of treatment compared with anastrozole alone.",,True,,"[{""registered_endpoint"": ""Percent Change From Baseline to 2 Weeks in Ki67 Expression"", ""published_corresponding_endpoint"": ""Decrease in Ki67 expression after 2 weeks of treatment"", ""same_construct"": true, ""same_timeframe"": true, ""same_definition"": true, ""same_population"": true, ""same_measurement_method"": null, ""registered_endpoint